# page-probe

Observation-only Darwin userspace program. Root's `build-page-probe.nix` is the libSystem-only recipe. Nothing in this directory was compiled or run here. A later desktop boot of the built binary is still required before any page-size value is known. The JSON record is not a pass or fail result, and it is not a guard-page fix.

`page-probe` prints one line of JSON to stdout. The record name is `OBSERVATION_ONLY`. Return codes, errnos, sizes, and widths are numbers.

## What it records

From the headers included by the program (`unistd.h`, `mach/mach.h`, `sys/mman.h`):

- `sizeof(vm_address_t)`, `sizeof(vm_size_t)`, and `sizeof(void *)`, as `sizeof_vm_address_t`, `sizeof_vm_size_t`, and `sizeof_void_star`. The program does not compare these with 4 or 8. `vm_address_t` is the header typedef `vm_offset_t`; the arm header selects `uintptr_t` or `natural_t` according to its own configuration. This file does not assume which one the included headers picked.
- `getpagesize()` and `sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE)`
- `host_page_size()`
- `vm_page_size`, `vm_page_mask`, `vm_page_shift`
- `vm_kernel_page_size`, `vm_kernel_page_mask`, `vm_kernel_page_shift`

There is no handwritten `extern`. `mach/mach_interface.h` declares `vm_protect`. `mach_vm_protect` is not called.

It maps one private anonymous region of 65536 bytes (`MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANON`). `_DARWIN_C_SOURCE` is set so that public `MAP_ANON` declaration is visible. Inside that region it takes a 16384-byte-aligned address and, for offsets 0, 4096, and 8192, length 16384, records:

- `vm_protect` with `VM_PROT_NONE` and `set_maximum` false
- `mprotect` with `PROT_NONE`

After every one of those attempts, and once more before `munmap`, the whole 65536-byte mapping is set back to read/write through both calls. `set_maximum` stays false. The program never loads or stores through the mapping.

## Guard on every `vm_protect`

`checked_vm_protect` is the only function that calls `vm_protect`. It checks the real pointer first. The span must be non-empty and entirely inside the one anonymous region. An address that would wrap when the 16384-byte base is formed is not used.

If the span is inside, the helper then requires both round trips:

- `(uintptr_t)(vm_address_t)address == address`
- `(size_t)(vm_size_t)length == length`

The Mach call uses the values that passed. If either comparison fails, the record has `called` 0 and `abi_truncation` 1, and `vm_protect` is not called. `ret` and `errno` are omitted on that record so a skip cannot be read as `KERN_SUCCESS`. `mprotect` of that same in-range span still runs, with the original `void *` and `size_t`.

A span outside the anonymous region produces `called` 0 and `outside_region` 1 for that attempt. Neither `vm_protect` nor `mprotect` is invoked for it. The truncation check is not applied to an outside span.

## Probe `kind`

| kind | call |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | `vm_protect` of one subspan to `VM_PROT_NONE` |
| 2 | `mprotect` of one subspan to `PROT_NONE` |
| 3 | `vm_protect` of the whole mapping back to read/write |
| 4 | `mprotect` of the whole mapping back to read/write |

`whole` is 1 for a full-mapping restore and 0 for a subspan. `called` 1 means that call was made, and `ret` and `errno` are present. `called` 0 means it was not made. `errno` is sampled only immediately after a call that ran.

`base_inside` is present when `mmap` succeeds. The offset probes run only when that aligned base lies inside the mapping. A failed `mmap` leaves `probes` empty and omits the base fields. The process still exits 0.

`commpage_read` is 0. The probe does not dereference a commpage byte. The page-size globals above are the values libSystem already published.

## What it does not do

It does not set `MallocGuardEdges`, `MallocDoNotProtectPostlude`, or any other malloc policy. It does not hide the existing `mvm_protect` warning. Libmalloc may still print that warning on the console before this JSON line. That line is not part of the JSON object.

It does not patch the kernel, change guard pages, or read protected memory.

## Build and run

`build-page-probe.nix` is root's source-only recipe. It links libSystem and allows the `getpagesize` deprecation warning to remain a warning under `-Werror`. This agent did not run that recipe, and this directory's source is not a boot result.
